WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.802 --> 00:00:14.563
My name is Shari Davis,

00:00:14.587 --> 00:00:15.874
and let's be honest,

00:00:15.898 --> 00:00:17.898
I'm a recovering government employee.

00:00:18.722 --> 00:00:22.594
And I say that with a huge shout-out
to the folks that work in government

00:00:22.618 --> 00:00:24.474
and on systems change.

00:00:24.498 --> 00:00:25.839
It's hard.

00:00:25.863 --> 00:00:27.609
It can be isolating.

00:00:27.633 --> 00:00:30.233
And the work can feel impossible.

00:00:30.971 --> 00:00:33.796
But government is the people that show up.

00:00:34.614 --> 00:00:37.498
Really, it's the people that can show up

00:00:37.522 --> 00:00:41.085
and are committed to the promise
that public service offers:

00:00:41.109 --> 00:00:42.839
service to people,

00:00:42.863 --> 00:00:44.237
democracy

00:00:44.261 --> 00:00:47.847
and fixing the problems
that community members face.

00:00:48.839 --> 00:00:50.197
Seventeen years ago,

00:00:50.221 --> 00:00:54.233
I walked through city hall
for the first time as a staff member.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:57.002
And that walk revealed something to me.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:00:59.598
I was a unicorn.

00:00:59.622 --> 00:01:02.109
There weren't many people
who looked like me

00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:03.482
that worked in the building.

00:01:03.871 --> 00:01:07.899
And yet, there were folks committed
to addressing hundreds of years

00:01:07.923 --> 00:01:09.639
of systemic inequity

00:01:09.663 --> 00:01:12.987
that left some behind and many ignored.

00:01:13.011 --> 00:01:14.998
Where there was promise,

00:01:15.022 --> 00:01:16.910
there was a huge problem.

00:01:16.934 --> 00:01:20.370
You see, democracy,
as it was originally designed,

00:01:20.394 --> 00:01:21.720
had a fatal flaw.

00:01:22.188 --> 00:01:27.640
It only laid pipeline
for rich white men to progress.

00:01:27.958 --> 00:01:31.091
And now, if you're a smart rich white man,

00:01:31.115 --> 00:01:34.480
you understand why I say that's a problem.

00:01:34.504 --> 00:01:37.845
Massive talent has been left
off the field.

00:01:38.201 --> 00:01:41.788
Our moral imaginations have grown anemic.

00:01:41.812 --> 00:01:45.760
Our highest offices
are plagued by corruption.

00:01:45.784 --> 00:01:50.122
We're on the brink
of a sort of apathetic apocalypse,

00:01:50.146 --> 00:01:52.939
and it's not OK.

00:01:52.963 --> 00:01:54.598
We've got to open the doors

00:01:54.622 --> 00:01:56.014
to city halls and schools

00:01:56.038 --> 00:01:59.800
so wide that people
can't help but walk in.

00:02:00.158 --> 00:02:03.245
We've got to throw out
the old top-down processes

00:02:03.269 --> 00:02:05.150
that got us into this mess,

00:02:05.174 --> 00:02:06.682
and start over,

00:02:06.706 --> 00:02:09.060
with new faces around the table,

00:02:09.084 --> 00:02:11.115
new voices in the mix,

00:02:11.139 --> 00:02:15.607
and we have to welcome new perspectives
every step of the way.

00:02:15.631 --> 00:02:18.108
Not because it's the right thing to do --

00:02:18.132 --> 00:02:19.647
although it is --

00:02:19.671 --> 00:02:24.005
but because that's the only way
for us to all succeed together.

00:02:24.355 --> 00:02:26.355
And here's the best news of all.

00:02:26.934 --> 00:02:28.514
I know how to do it.

00:02:28.538 --> 00:02:31.164
The answer -- well, an answer,

00:02:31.188 --> 00:02:33.482
is participatory budgeting.

00:02:33.839 --> 00:02:35.085
That's right.

00:02:35.109 --> 00:02:39.346
Participatory budgeting,
or "PB" for short.

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:43.163
PB is a process that brings
community and government together

00:02:43.187 --> 00:02:46.687
to ideate, develop concrete proposals

00:02:46.711 --> 00:02:50.826
and vote on projects
that solve real problems in community.

00:02:51.263 --> 00:02:54.334
Now I realize that people
don't get up and dance

00:02:54.358 --> 00:02:56.755
when I start talking about public budgets.

00:02:57.096 --> 00:02:59.458
But participatory budgeting

00:02:59.482 --> 00:03:03.918
is actually about collective,
radical imagination.

00:03:04.474 --> 00:03:07.134
Everyone has a role to play in PB,

00:03:07.158 --> 00:03:08.441
and it works,

00:03:08.465 --> 00:03:12.998
because it allows community members
to craft real solutions

00:03:13.022 --> 00:03:14.514
to real problems

00:03:14.538 --> 00:03:19.018
and provides the infrastructure
for the promise of government.

00:03:19.042 --> 00:03:20.193
And honestly,

00:03:20.217 --> 00:03:24.740
it's how I saw a democracy
actually work for the first time.

00:03:25.177 --> 00:03:27.420
I remember it like it was yesterday.

00:03:27.444 --> 00:03:29.849
It was 2014 in Boston, Massachusetts,

00:03:29.873 --> 00:03:31.676
and mayor Menino asked me

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:35.225
to launch the country's first
youth-focused PB effort

00:03:35.249 --> 00:03:37.923
with one million dollars of city funds.

00:03:38.312 --> 00:03:41.312
Now, we didn't start
with line items and limits

00:03:41.336 --> 00:03:43.125
or spreadsheets and formulas.

00:03:43.149 --> 00:03:44.712
We started with people.

00:03:45.149 --> 00:03:48.702
We wanted to make sure
that everyone was listened to.

00:03:48.726 --> 00:03:50.132
So we brought in young people

00:03:50.156 --> 00:03:53.734
from historically and traditionally
marginalized neighborhoods,

00:03:53.758 --> 00:03:55.815
members of the queer community

00:03:55.839 --> 00:03:57.871
and youth that were formerly incarcerated,

00:03:57.895 --> 00:04:02.320
and together, often with pizza
and a sugar-free beverage,

00:04:02.344 --> 00:04:04.915
we talked about how to make Boston better.

00:04:04.939 --> 00:04:09.066
And we designed a process that we called
"Youth Lead the Change."

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.349
We imagined a Boston

00:04:11.373 --> 00:04:14.333
where young people
could access the information

00:04:14.357 --> 00:04:16.404
that they need to thrive.

00:04:16.428 --> 00:04:19.902
Where they could feel safe
in their communities,

00:04:19.926 --> 00:04:24.791
and where they can transform public spaces
into real hubs of life

00:04:24.815 --> 00:04:26.033
for all people.

00:04:26.339 --> 00:04:28.299
And that's exactly what they did.

00:04:28.323 --> 00:04:29.760
In the first year,

00:04:29.784 --> 00:04:35.601
young people allocated 90,000 dollars
to increase technology access

00:04:35.625 --> 00:04:37.772
for Boston public high school students,

00:04:37.796 --> 00:04:41.280
by delivering laptops
right to Boston public high schools,

00:04:41.304 --> 00:04:45.567
so that students could thrive
inside and outside of the classroom.

00:04:45.928 --> 00:04:49.325
They allocated 60,000 dollars
to creating art walls

00:04:49.349 --> 00:04:53.807
that literally and figuratively
brightened up public spaces.

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:56.529
But they addressed
a more important problem.

00:04:56.553 --> 00:05:00.799
Young people were being criminalized
and pulled into the justice system

00:05:00.823 --> 00:05:04.125
for putting their art on walls.

00:05:04.149 --> 00:05:07.947
So this gave them a safe space
to practice their craft.

00:05:08.249 --> 00:05:12.376
They allocated 400,000 dollars
to renovating parks,

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:16.874
to make them more accessible
for all people of all bodies.

00:05:16.898 --> 00:05:18.898
Now, admittedly,

00:05:18.922 --> 00:05:22.183
this didn't go as smoothly
as we had planned.

00:05:22.676 --> 00:05:24.876
Right before we broke ground on the park,

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:28.363
we actually found out that it was on top
of an archaeological site

00:05:28.387 --> 00:05:30.387
and had to halt construction.

00:05:31.117 --> 00:05:32.867
I thought I broke PB.

00:05:33.241 --> 00:05:37.328
But because the city
was so committed to the project,

00:05:37.352 --> 00:05:38.780
that's not what happened.

00:05:38.804 --> 00:05:41.783
They invited community in to do a dig,

00:05:41.807 --> 00:05:43.601
protected the site,

00:05:43.625 --> 00:05:45.143
found artifacts,

00:05:45.167 --> 00:05:47.680
extended Boston's history

00:05:47.704 --> 00:05:50.267
and then moved forward
with the renovation.

00:05:50.942 --> 00:05:55.164
If that isn't a reflection
of radical imagination in government,

00:05:55.188 --> 00:05:57.069
I don't know what is.

00:05:57.093 --> 00:05:59.180
What sounds simple

00:05:59.204 --> 00:06:03.886
is actually transformational
for the people and communities involved.

00:06:04.307 --> 00:06:08.490
I'm seeing community members
shape transportation access,

00:06:08.514 --> 00:06:10.136
improve their schools

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:12.398
and even transform government buildings,

00:06:12.422 --> 00:06:15.683
so that there is space
inside of them for them.

00:06:15.707 --> 00:06:17.514
Before we had PB,

00:06:17.538 --> 00:06:19.958
I would see people who look like me

00:06:19.982 --> 00:06:21.523
and come from where I come from

00:06:21.547 --> 00:06:24.824
walk in to government buildings
for this new initiative

00:06:24.848 --> 00:06:26.609
or that new working group,

00:06:26.633 --> 00:06:29.172
and then I'd watch them
walk right back out.

00:06:29.641 --> 00:06:31.934
Sometimes I wouldn't see them again.

00:06:32.379 --> 00:06:36.498
It's because their expertise
was being unvalued.

00:06:36.522 --> 00:06:39.820
They weren't truly
being engaged in the process.

00:06:39.844 --> 00:06:42.494
Put PB is different.

00:06:42.876 --> 00:06:44.176
When we started doing PB,

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:47.437
I met amazing young leaders
across the city.

00:06:47.961 --> 00:06:52.017
One in particular, a rock star,
Malachi Hernandez,

00:06:52.041 --> 00:06:53.485
15 years old,

00:06:53.509 --> 00:06:55.111
came into a community meeting --

00:06:55.135 --> 00:06:58.180
shy, curious, a little quiet.

00:06:58.204 --> 00:06:59.704
Stuck around

00:06:59.728 --> 00:07:02.656
and became one of the young people
hoping to lead the project.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:05.220
Now fast-forward a couple of years.

00:07:05.561 --> 00:07:09.910
Malachi was the first in his family
to attend college.

00:07:09.934 --> 00:07:11.283
A couple of weeks ago,

00:07:11.307 --> 00:07:14.569
he was the first
in his family to graduate.

00:07:14.966 --> 00:07:16.688
Malachi has appeared

00:07:16.712 --> 00:07:19.355
in the Obama White House several times

00:07:19.379 --> 00:07:21.931
as part of the My Brother's
Keeper initiative.

00:07:21.955 --> 00:07:26.431
President Obama even quotes
Malachi in interviews.

00:07:26.455 --> 00:07:28.832
It's true, you can look it up.

00:07:28.856 --> 00:07:31.934
Malachi got engaged, stayed engaged,

00:07:31.958 --> 00:07:36.336
and is out here changing the way
we think about community leadership

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:37.812
and potential.

00:07:37.836 --> 00:07:39.629
Or my friend Maria Hadden,

00:07:39.653 --> 00:07:43.200
who was involved
in the first PB process in Chicago.

00:07:43.595 --> 00:07:45.509
Then went on to become a founding

00:07:45.533 --> 00:07:48.412
participatory budgeting
project board member,

00:07:48.436 --> 00:07:50.458
eventually a staff member,

00:07:50.482 --> 00:07:53.649
and then unseated a 28-year incumbent,

00:07:53.673 --> 00:07:56.879
becoming the first queer Black alderperson

00:07:56.903 --> 00:07:59.300
in Chicago's history.

00:07:59.641 --> 00:08:01.641
That's real engagement.

00:08:02.022 --> 00:08:04.188
That's being taken seriously.

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:08.067
That's building out and building on
community leadership.

00:08:08.546 --> 00:08:10.569
That's system change.

00:08:10.593 --> 00:08:12.950
And it's not just in the US either.

00:08:12.974 --> 00:08:15.585
After starting 30 years ago in Brazil,

00:08:15.609 --> 00:08:19.774
PB has spread to over 7,000 cities
across the globe.

00:08:20.107 --> 00:08:21.481
In Paris, France,

00:08:21.505 --> 00:08:24.393
the mayor puts up
five percent of her budget,

00:08:24.417 --> 00:08:26.561
over 100 million euros,

00:08:26.585 --> 00:08:30.533
for community members to decide on
and shape their city.

00:08:30.950 --> 00:08:36.062
Globally, PB has been shown
to improve public health,

00:08:36.086 --> 00:08:37.554
reduce corruption

00:08:37.578 --> 00:08:39.894
and increase trust in government.

00:08:40.331 --> 00:08:44.180
Now we know the challenges
that we face in today's society.

00:08:44.871 --> 00:08:48.005
How can we expect people
to feel motivated,

00:08:48.029 --> 00:08:49.656
to show up to the polls

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:54.863
when they can't trust that government
is run by and for the people.

00:08:55.482 --> 00:08:58.409
I argue that we haven't
actually experienced

00:08:58.433 --> 00:09:00.545
true participatory democracy

00:09:00.569 --> 00:09:03.760
in these United States
of America just yet.

00:09:03.784 --> 00:09:06.967
But democracy is a living,
breathing thing.

00:09:07.409 --> 00:09:10.226
And it's still our birthright.

00:09:10.655 --> 00:09:14.403
It's time to renew trust,
and that's not going to come easy.

00:09:14.427 --> 00:09:16.717
We have to build new ways of thinking,

00:09:16.741 --> 00:09:20.622
of talking, of working,
of dreaming, of planning

00:09:20.646 --> 00:09:21.891
in its place.

00:09:21.915 --> 00:09:26.807
What would America look like
if everyone had a seat at the table?

00:09:26.831 --> 00:09:30.768
If we took the time to reimagine
what's possible,

00:09:30.792 --> 00:09:34.067
and then ask, "How do we get there?"

00:09:34.593 --> 00:09:38.855
My favorite author,
Octavia Butler, says it best.

00:09:39.188 --> 00:09:42.910
In "Parable of the Sower,"
basically my Bible, she says,

00:09:42.934 --> 00:09:46.483
"All that you touch
You Change.

00:09:46.507 --> 00:09:49.790
All that you Change
Changes you.

00:09:49.814 --> 00:09:53.750
The only lasting truth
Is Change.

00:09:53.774 --> 00:09:56.180
God
Is Change."

00:09:56.204 --> 00:10:00.561
It's time for these 50 states to change.

00:10:00.585 --> 00:10:04.820
What got us here sure as hell
won't get us there.

00:10:04.844 --> 00:10:07.129
We've got to kick the walls of power down

00:10:07.153 --> 00:10:11.745
and plant gardens of genuine
democracy in their place.

00:10:11.769 --> 00:10:14.547
That's how we change systems.

00:10:14.571 --> 00:10:16.682
By opening doors so wide

00:10:16.706 --> 00:10:19.348
that people can't help but walk in.

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:21.825
So what's stopping you

00:10:21.849 --> 00:10:25.769
from bringing participatory budgeting
to your community?

